$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>A short marriage and a long case</h2>
<p>The parties married in August 2007 under the Special Marriage Act and separated within months. There followed the familiar procedural archaeology of a broken marriage: a restitution suit by the husband, later withdrawn; a divorce suit dismissed for default; a complaint under Section 498A IPC; proceedings under the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act; and, in 2014, the wife\'s application under Section 125 of the Code of Criminal Procedure — the provision now carried forward as Section 144 of the BNSS. In March 2018 the Family Court awarded her Rs 25,000 per month with Rs 20,000 in litigation costs. The husband\'s revision against that order is what the High Court decided this month, eight years later.</p>

<h2>The competing cases</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><strong>The husband</strong> said he earns about Rs 45,000 a month, supports a dependent mother, and that the wife is qualified, has property, and left the matrimonial home of her own accord before delaying her claim for years.</div>
<div class="arrow">⇄</div>
<div class="col new"><strong>The wife</strong> said she was driven out within months of the marriage, cannot earn because of a hand injury, and that the husband suppressed his income, produced nothing, and abandoned the trial midway — inviting an adverse presumption under Section 114(g) of the Evidence Act.</div>
</div>

<h2>What the High Court held</h2>
<p>Justice Chaitali Chatterjee Das identified the defect at the root of the award: the Family Court had ascertained neither the husband\'s actual income nor the wife\'s entitlement. The order was, in the Court\'s words, absolutely silent about the entitlement of the opposite party — yet the sine qua non of a Section 125 application is that the wife is unable to maintain herself. Quantum had, in effect, been plucked from the air. The Court added a sentence that deserves a place in every maintenance brief: the legislature never intended the provision as a tool of punishment for the husband. Section 125 is a measure of social justice directed at preventing destitution, not a penal tariff on a failed marriage.</p>

<p>At the same time, the Court did not accept the husband\'s invitation to simply dismiss the claim. The defect was in the fact-finding, not necessarily in the result, and the remedy for missing findings is a remand. The Magistrate must rehear the application afresh, with both parties filing affidavits of assets and liabilities in the <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em> format and adducing evidence, and must complete the exercise without further adjournments.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The interim arrangement is the part litigants should study. Pending the fresh decision, the husband pays Rs 10,000 per month by the 7th of each month, and sums already paid are to be adjusted against the final outcome. A remand therefore did not become a payment holiday — a point the Court underlined by noticing the husband\'s earlier arrest for non-compliance.</p>
</div>

<h2>Lessons for both sides</h2>
<p>For claimants, the judgment is a reminder that entitlement must be proved, not presumed: plead and establish the inability to maintain oneself, explain any long delay in approaching the court, and file a complete assets-and-liabilities affidavit, because a quantum unsupported by findings is vulnerable years later in revision. For respondents, the case shows both the power and the limits of the income attack: suppression of income and abandonment of the trial invite adverse presumptions, and even a successful revision will ordinarily produce a remand with interim payments — not an escape from the obligation. The Delhi courts apply the same architecture under Section 144 BNSS, with the <em>Rajnesh</em> affidavits as the evidentiary spine of every contested maintenance case.</p>
